```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // Check for special cases
    if (n <= 0 || (n & (n - 1)) != 0) { // Not a power of two
        return false;
    }

    int exponent = 0;
    while (n >= 3 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0) { // Shift right until it's no longer divisible by 3
        n >>>= 1;
        exponent++;
    }
    return n == 1 ? (exponent % 3 == 0) : false;
}
```

The idea behind this solution is as follows:

- A number `n` can be expressed as a power of three if and only if `log3(n)` (the logarithm base 3 of `n`) is an integer.
- Since 3 is a prime number, a number `n` is a power of three if and only if it's a power of two multiplied by 3 (or 9, or 27, etc.).
- If `n` is a power of two, then its binary representation contains only one `1`, shifted to some index `k`. By subtracting `1` from `n`, we remove the last significant bit without changing the other bits (which ensures that `n & (n - 1)` equals zero for any power of two), so checking whether `n & (n - 1) == 0` detects whether `n` is a power of two.
- Now suppose `n` has `m` trailing zeros in its binary representation (i.e., `n` can be written as `2^k * (1 + 2 + ... + 2^(m-1))`). We shift right by `m` positions (equivalent to dividing by `2^m`) and increment the exponent keeping track of how many times `2` was divided to reduce the number of trailing zeros. Eventually, when `n` becomes odd (not multiple of 3 anymore), we compare the exponent with `3` multiples to check if `n` can still be written as `3^k`.

The time complexity of this solution is O(1), space complexity is also O(1).

